# SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <croi/syscall.h>

/* CROI_SYS_HANDLE_CLOSE */
int64_t handle_close(const uint32_t *handles, uint64_t count);
/* CROI_SYS_HANDLE_DUPLICATE */
int64_t handle_duplicate(croi_handle_duplicate_item_t *items, uint64_t count);

int64_t croi_handle_close(uint32_t handle);
int64_t croi_handle_duplicate(uint32_t handle, uint32_t rights, uint32_t *out,
                              uint32_t options);
```

# DESCRIPTION

`handle_close` closes <count> handles, the values read from the array <handles>.
A value of 0 is skipped.
The handles are read and closed 64 at a time.
A value that is not a handle is remembered, the rest are still closed, and the call then fails with `CROI_ERR_BAD_HANDLE`.
An array that cannot be read stops the call at that chunk with `CROI_ERR_INVALID_ARGS`; the chunks before it are closed.
It returns 0 otherwise.
Closing a handle drops its reference to the object, which is destroyed when the last reference goes.
Bindings and waits made through the handle (port(2)) end.
Closing the handle of a PMT without unpinning it quarantines its pages; see bti(2).
`croi_handle_close` closes one.

`handle_duplicate` makes a new handle in the caller's table for each of up to 64 items:

```c
typedef struct {
  uint32_t handle;    // the handle to duplicate
  uint32_t rights;    // a subset of its rights, or CROI_RIGHT_SAME_RIGHTS
  uint32_t options;   // 0 or CROI_HANDLE_DUPLICATE_CONSUME
  uint32_t out;       // out: the new handle
  int32_t status;     // out: 0, or why this item failed
  uint32_t reserved;  // 0
} croi_handle_duplicate_item_t;
```

With <options> 0 the handle needs `CROI_RIGHT_DUPLICATE`, and stays.
With `CROI_HANDLE_DUPLICATE_CONSUME` the handle is replaced: it needs no DUPLICATE right and is closed, even when the item fails.
Either way <rights> must be a subset of the handle's, or `CROI_RIGHT_SAME_RIGHTS` for all of them; anything else is `CROI_ERR_INVALID_ARGS`.
An <options> other than those two, or a nonzero <reserved>, is `CROI_ERR_INVALID_ARGS`.
Items are done in order and the call stops at the first that fails.
It returns how many succeeded; each processed item has its <status> and, on success, its <out> written.
This is how a program narrows the rights of a handle before it sends it.

`croi_handle_duplicate` does one item and returns 0, the item's status or the call's.

